[17592186480591 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480591 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480591 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a97f6f8c-e126-5e61-838a-7e75c12192eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480598 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480598 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480598 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480598 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480598 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480598 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480598 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480598 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480598 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c51adab7-d9bc-58f1-9bfb-406f118e6c90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480601 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480601 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480601 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480601 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480601 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480601 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480601 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480601 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480601 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c9c885db-a51b-565f-802d-b5e3e3429fb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480643 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480643 #edb/ref 1069 "q-353476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480643 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480643 #edb/ref 1071 "The number $y(t)$ of people in a closed community who have been infected with a virus follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2400y-3{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=12,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of infected people?\n\nThe maximum possible number of infected people is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480643 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "21fb3dfd-c1e0-5974-85b5-71a08b2e3884" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480643 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of people who will be infected. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2400y-3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3y(800-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=800.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $800,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of infected people is $800.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480644 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480644 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480644 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480644 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,500y-12{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=15\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480644 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2304365e-e658-571a-a752-03df743e6d8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480644 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=12y(125-y),\\,y(0)=15.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=125.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $125.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480660 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480660 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480660 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480660 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,800y-2{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=30\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480660 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "548860d7-a3cd-5a87-9ba4-81882cafa76b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480660 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y(900-y),\\,y(0)=30.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=900.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $900.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480661 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480661 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480661 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480661 #edb/ref 1071 "The population $y(t)$ of deer in a certain forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.6y-0.0012{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=120,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of deer?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480661 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c2c81b1-ac73-5714-9490-1d6081f1f637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480661 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.6y-0.001\\,2{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0012y(500-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=500.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $500,$ and we conclude that the number of deer cannot exceed $500.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480671 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480671 #edb/ref 1069 "q-353491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480671 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480671 #edb/ref 1071 "The number $P(t)$ of employees at a growing company follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=9600P-8{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=150,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the number of employees growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480671 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "780f42b2-e05c-5d59-ba5e-7cf6b84ac19f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480671 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/90/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =9600P-8{P}^{2} \\\\  & =8P(1200-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1200.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1200,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1200=600.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480676 #edb/ref 1069 "q-353469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480676 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480676 #edb/ref 1071 "The number $y(t)$ of homes in a city that have adopted clean energy follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y(20-\\frac{y}{125}),\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy?\n\nThe maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480676 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "990eaa9e-cd8b-5349-b27e-24596fc286e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480676 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of clean-energy-adopting homes in the city. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y(20-\\frac{y}{125}) \\\\  & =3y(\\frac{2\\,500-y}{125}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{125}(2\\,500-y) \\\\  & =0.024y(2\\,500-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2500.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2500,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy is $2500.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480677 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480677 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118575" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480677 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480677 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=900y-6{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=35\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480677 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99513b0e-90cb-593a-b1cd-0a535510b802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480677 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6y(150-y),\\,y(0)=35.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=150.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $150.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480683 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85982" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480683 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480683 #edb/ref 1071 "The population $P(t)$ of turtles  on a certain island follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=4.8P-0.001\\,2{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=200,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the turtle population growing at the fastest possible rate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480683 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a97fa6ce-6558-5992-afd2-fa81f89839cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480683 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4.8P-0.001\\,2{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.001\\,2P(4\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=4\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $4\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\,000=2\\,000.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480697 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480697 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480697 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480697 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y=y(t):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9y(1-\\frac{y}{1\\,200}),\\,y(0)=200\n$$\n\nWhat is ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480697 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da1f1140-0b6f-5e8e-98c1-c5bc7c260a47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480697 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9y(1-\\frac{y}{1\\,200}) \\\\  & =9y(\\frac{1\\,200-y}{1\\,200}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9y}{1\\,200}(1\\,200-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{400}(1\\,200-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,200.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,200.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)=1\\,200.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
